\section{Lower the Contract to Two Stages}

This is Verilog-2005 \cite{ieee1364verilog}, saved as \texttt{byte\_pipe.v}. The port assignments are unsigned, with an eight-bit truncation at each data register.

\begin{verbatim}
module byte_pipe(input clk, rst, vin,
                 input [7:0] a, b,
                 output reg vout, output reg [7:0] y);
    reg v1;
    reg [7:0] triple, bias;
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (rst) begin
            v1 <= 0; vout <= 0;
            triple <= 0; bias <= 0; y <= 0;
        end else begin
            v1 <= vin;
            if (vin) begin
                triple <= (a << 1) + a;
                bias <= b;
            end
            vout <= v1;
            if (v1) y <= triple + bias;
        end
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

The separate reference algorithm keeps a full-width mathematical result and only narrows at comparison. This pseudocode describes a post-edge scoreboard:

\begin{verbatim}
pending = NONE
for each sampled edge:
    if rst:
        pending = NONE           # flush, including the prior sample
        assert vout == 0
    else:
        assert vout == has_value(pending)
        if pending exists: assert y == pending mod 256
        pending = 3*a + b if vin else NONE  # advance after comparison
\end{verbatim}

If \(a=200\), \(b=230\), then \(3a=600\), the first stage stores \(88\), and the second stores \((88+230)\bmod256=62\). The full calculation \(830\bmod256=62\) agrees because adding multiples of \(256\) cannot change the low byte. If that pair is followed by a bubble, it still produces a valid \(62\); a reset instead of the bubble suppresses it. \Cref{fig:visual-322} shows the path of this transaction.

