\section{Compare the Mapped Design Against a Reference}

Use the same ports for the independent reference, and put the complete arithmetic expression before the first register. This is \texttt{byte\_ref.v}:

\begin{verbatim}
module byte_ref(input clk, rst, vin,
                input [7:0] a, b,
                output reg vout, output reg [7:0] y);
    wire [9:0] full = 10'd3 * {2'b0,a} + {2'b0,b};
    reg v1;
    reg [9:0] pending;
    always @(posedge clk) begin
        if (rst) begin
            v1 <= 0; vout <= 0; pending <= 0; y <= 0;
        end else begin
            v1 <= vin;
            pending <= full;
            vout <= v1;
            if (v1) y <= pending[7:0];
        end
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

Ten bits suffice since \(3(255)+255=1020<1024\). Unlike the scoreboard, these reference outputs also specify hold-on-bubble behavior, which is extra behavior both versions implement. The following shell commands use Yosys and the installed iCE40 primitive simulation models. Start the comparison in a fresh Yosys process so only the mapped design supplies \texttt{byte\_pipe}.

\begin{verbatim}
yosys -p 'read_verilog byte_pipe.v;
  synth_ice40 -top byte_pipe -json byte_pipe.json;
  write_verilog -noattr byte_pipe_mapped.v'
yosys -p 'read_verilog -nowb +/ice40/cells_sim.v;
  read_verilog byte_pipe_mapped.v byte_ref.v;
  miter -equiv -make_assert byte_ref byte_pipe compare;
  prep -top compare -flatten; opt;
  sat -verify -seq 5 -set-at 1 in_rst 1 -prove-asserts -prove-skip 1'
\end{verbatim}

The generated Verilog keeps the target cells and their parameter values; \texttt{-nowb} lets the proof flatten the behavioral primitive models. The SAT check forces reset in its first step and compares outputs in subsequent steps, allowing arbitrary later resets, data and bubbles. With YoWASP Yosys 0.71 it returned \texttt{SUCCESS}. This is bounded logical equivalence under the primitive models, not proof of electrical behavior. An unbounded proof can instead maintain equality of the validity bits and, whenever \texttt{v1} is high, equality of the pending low byte with the sum of the stored operands modulo \(256\). This one-clock case has no CDC; a later wrapper that crosses \texttt{vin}, \(a\), or \(b\) must add a complete crossing protocol rather than synchronize the bits independently.

\section{Route a Device, Not a Board}

For a laboratory exercise without hardware, the shell flow can continue with nextpnr and IceStorm:

\begin{verbatim}
nextpnr-ice40 --hx8k --package ct256 --freq 12 \
  --json byte_pipe.json --asc byte_pipe.asc \
  --report byte_pipe_report.json
icepack byte_pipe.asc byte_pipe.bin
\end{verbatim}

The \href{https://github.com/YosysHQ/nextpnr/blob/main/docs/constraints.md}{nextpnr constraints guide} explains the frequency target and PCF format. Here \texttt{--freq 12} is an implementation target, not evidence that every input and output has a board-level timing contract. Here the package pins are not assigned against a board schematic. Treat the route and report as an implementation exercise; do not program the binary on a board. For that use, supply an actual PCF, electrical constraints and a verified board clock and I/O interface, then confirm in \texttt{byte\_pipe\_report.json} that routing completed and the constrained clock meets both applicable timing checks. The ASC describes routed configuration; the binary encoding is a separate device-dependent artifact. Neither is an ASIC layout.

The structural tradeoff is clear without a timing claim: moving the second add past a register requires preserving the bias and its validity. Actual delay depends on carry resources, enable logic, and routing. Use the report to locate the critical endpoints; assume neither that the first stage is critical nor that the arithmetic is the limiting path. Compare alternative pipelines only under matching interfaces and implementation constraints.
